VulkanLumen — Stage 5: world-space directional radiance cache

The complete Lumen implementation continues to live in vulkan-systems/lumen/.
vulkan-experiment remains the same Vulkan window/camera/frame test harness,
with only its terminal status and per-pass GPU profiling labels updated.

Scene:
  - Original 16-face Cornell room and two rotated blocks
  - Full-resolution primary raster visibility at capped internal resolution
  - 30-triangle/59-node static VulkanBVH-compatible ray traversal
  - One deterministic BVH shadow ray per visible fragment
  - Stage 4 material cards: 16 x 64x64, packed in one RGBA8 256x256 atlas

Stage 5 world-space GI:
  - A fixed 12x8x12 grid (1152 world-space probes), device-local SSBO
  - Each probe stores directional radiance as four RGB first-order spherical
    harmonic coefficients: mean radiance and X/Y/Z directional moments
  - A bounded round-robin selection updates a fraction of probes per frame
    (settings.probe_updates_per_frame, default 64, valid 1..1152)
  - Each updated probe traces settings.rays_per_probe (default 4, valid 1..16)
    stratified directions across the sphere through the same BVH as shadows
  - Hits fetch albedo from the material cards and evaluate directly reflected
    ceiling area light with BVH-tested visibility
  - The resulting radiance is ONE secondary diffuse bounce: it does not
    redundantly include the primary receiver's direct light
  - Probe coefficients accumulate over time with a bounded history of 32
    probe updates. No per-pixel temporal denoising or screen blur
  - Surface shading trilinearly blends its nearest valid eight probes with
    a normal-facing bias and integrates the directional field for the
    receiver normal, multiplying by the receiver albedo
  - Probes inside either Cornell block are invalidated; no fake GI values
  - Probe creation, GPU memory synchronization and cache updates are confined
    to VulkanLumen::record(). The full scene/host API is unchanged
  - On invalidate(), the material cache is refreshed and probe history is
    cleared. Resize() rebuilds all owned GPU resources
  - The material cache is rebuilt only when dirty; the probe field refreshes
    incrementally every frame even if the camera does not move

Profiling:
  GPU timestamps now separately report material cache, radiance cache
  compute, scene raster + shadows, composite and total.
  CPU-side FPS and max/average frame times are unchanged.
  GI should become visible after one complete probe sweep. With the default
  64 updates, 1152 probes take 18 frames (~0.3 seconds at 60 FPS) for first
  coverage; radiance converges progressively for subsequent seconds.

Important limitations:
  - This is a small first-order directional probe cache, NOT yet Epic's full
    Lumen world-space radiance cache, surface-lighting atlas or DDGI solution.
  - No directional depth-moment visibility, relocation, global SDF,
    screen-probe gather or true multibounce transport yet. Normal facing and
    invalidating interior probes reduce but do not eliminate light leaks.
  - Only the internal Cornell scene is traced; generic set_scene mesh handles
    and external G-buffers remain unimplemented.
  - The public outputs.diffuse image remains null; diffuse GI currently
    contributes within the Cornell material shader.
  - No reflection tracing and no denoisers.
  - The grid bounds and face count are Cornell-demo constants.
  - The program may display weak GI for the initial 18 frames during
    initial probe coverage, followed by progressively more stable color bleed.
  - No performance guarantee on the base M1; compare the new GPU GI/scene
    times against the 16.67ms frame budget.

Test from vulkan-experiment/
  git pull --ff-only
  rm -f c.lock
  c build run vulkan-experiment

Look for:
  build/shaders/lumen_radiance.comp
  [lumen] Stage 5 ... world probes=1152 updates/frame=64 rays/update=4 ...
  [lumen] ... GPU cards=... GI=... scene=... composite=... total=...

Expected visual change: red/green light bouncing onto the nearby white
floor and boxes without a screen-space denoiser. Send a screenshot and
several post-warmup timing lines before Stage 6 (screen probe gather).
